"> ⚠️ **Template notice — not part of the message. Do not copy this notice into your handoff.**
> Recipient visibility: the \`specifier\` agent receives only the text inside your \`handoff --next-role="specifier"\` command. It cannot see anything you write in this agent session, or tool output. Put your complete deliverable in the handoff message.

Handoff template \`builder\` → \`specifier\` (copy only the body sections below; do not copy this line, the notice, or the instructions after the body):

Instructions (not part of the message): complete every section below. Do not omit sections, principles, or XML wrappers.

When a section has no content, write exactly \`Not Applicable.\` — no explanation, no em-dash, no additional text.

\`\`\`markdown
## Summary
<what was implemented or attempted, in plain terms>

## Template Disclosure Confirmation
- [ ] I confirm that I have seen this template at the start of this task, before implementing or modifying any code
- [ ] I confirm that I've read and followed the role guidance before starting any work
<!-- Role guidance is static for your role and does not change between tasks. Run once if needed: \`chatroom get-role-guidance --chatroom-id="test-chatroom-id" --role="builder"\`. You do not need to re-read it on every task if you have already read it once. -->

## Proof of Principles
<!-- REQUIRED: Complete every principle below. Write an explanation for each, or write exactly "Not Applicable." with no explanation when the principle does not apply — do not omit this section or skip any principle bullet. -->
- **Semantic Consistency:** <how this work demonstrates semantic consistency, or exactly "Not Applicable.">
<!-- Semantic Consistency: the organization of the code, the code and the functionality of the code use a consistent and well maintained set of terms. -->

- **Organization & Maintainability:** <how this work demonstrates organization & maintainability, or exactly "Not Applicable.">
<!-- Organization & Maintainability: a small change in requirements should result in a small change in code in a small number of files and folders. -->

- **Reducing Optionality:** <how this work demonstrates reducing optionality, or exactly "Not Applicable.">
<!-- Reducing Optionality: code contains the minimum number of code paths to support the functionality required presently. -->

- **Static Evaluability and Provability:** <how this work demonstrates static evaluability and provability, or exactly "Not Applicable.">
<!-- Static Evaluability and Provability: the system's behavior should be provably correct by looking at the source code, then automated tests, then manual tests, in this order. -->

- **No Revisit:** <how this work demonstrates no revisit, or exactly "Not Applicable.">
<!-- No Revisit: implemented in a way so the user does not have to revisit this implementation again. -->

- **Leave It Better:** <how this work demonstrates leave it better, or exactly "Not Applicable.">
<!-- Leave It Better: leave the code in a slightly better state than before when touching files. -->

- **Documented Constraints:** <how this work demonstrates documented constraints, or exactly "Not Applicable.">
<!-- Documented Constraints: the code written should also have documentation in comments that indicate the constraints that the code satisfies. -->

## Proof of Completion
- [ ] I confirm the delegation brief is fully met: all (Required) files done, verified end-to-end, acceptance criteria pass
<!-- Reference the ## Goal and ## Requirements (acceptance criteria) sections from the specifier handoff you received. State the delegation goal and confirm it was achieved. -->
<!-- File references (clickable in workspace UI): use repo-relative paths with a file extension — e.g. \`apps/webapp/src/modules/chatroom/foo.ts\` or [apps/webapp/src/foo.ts](apps/webapp/src/foo.ts). Avoid absolute paths, file:// prefixes, and paths without / or extension. -->
- \`apps/webapp/src/path/to/file.ts\` — <what changed and why>
<evidence the goal was met — list every file you modified; for each behavior the brief's \`## Test seams\` section names a seam for: the test name, the seam it calls, the failure output observed before the implementation, and the passing output after>

## Code Change Verification
- [ ] I confirm that I have run typecheck and tests for the project (only required if code changes were made)

## Blockers / questions
<!-- REQUIRED. List blockers, or write exactly "Not Applicable." with no explanation. Do not omit this section. -->
<anything needing specifier decision>

## Notes for review
<!-- REQUIRED. List review notes, or write exactly "Not Applicable." with no explanation. Do not omit this section. -->
<specific areas for specifier to check; any test written at a seam other than the one the brief's \`## Test seams\` section named, and why>
\`\`\`"
`;

exports[`trio handoff template snapshots > trio non-specialist pairs (chat) > coordinator->specifier 1`] = `
"> ⚠️ **Template notice — not part of the message. Do not copy this notice into your handoff.**
> Recipient visibility: the \`specifier\` agent receives only the text inside your \`handoff --next-role="specifier"\` command. It cannot see anything you write in this agent session, or tool output. Put your complete deliverable in the handoff message.

Handoff template \`coordinator\` → \`specifier\` (copy only the body sections below; do not copy this line, the notice, or the instructions after the body):

Instructions (not part of the message): Delegate ONE work package to the \`specifier\`. The specifier turns it into file-level builder slices, drives the builder through every build pass, and hands one consolidated report back to you. Clear every required gate first (researcher, triage, architect, uiux-engineer, game-asset-designer) and include the decisions from their briefs, citing message ids — the specifier cannot contact those roles. Do not write file-level code instructions yourself; state outcomes, constraints, and acceptance criteria. Each package starts the specifier in a fresh session: include everything it needs, or name the message ids it must read with \`chatroom messages download\`.

**Delegation Brief** — paste into the handoff message. Include every field that applies. **Omit fields that do not apply** — do not write \`Not Applicable\` as filler.

\`\`\`markdown
## Summary
<the user's intent in plain terms and where this package fits in the larger task>

## Goal
<one sentence: the outcome this work package delivers>

## User requirements
<the user's requirements and constraints, quoted or paraphrased, each with its source message id>

## Inputs from specialists
<for each architect / researcher / triage / uiux-engineer / game-asset-designer brief this package depends on: message id and the decisions to adopt; or \`None.\`>
<!-- File references (clickable in workspace UI): use repo-relative paths with a file extension — e.g. \`apps/webapp/src/modules/chatroom/foo.ts\` or [apps/webapp/src/foo.ts](apps/webapp/src/foo.ts). Avoid absolute paths, file:// prefixes, and paths without / or extension. -->

## Scope
- In scope: <surfaces and behaviors>
- Out of scope: <surfaces the specifier and builder must not touch>

## Rework findings (prior round)
<message id of the specifier, reviewer, or verifier report and the blocking findings to fix (paste each with file and line where known); or \`None.\`>

## Acceptance criteria
- <verifiable outcome; include at least one end-to-end check>

## Delivery expectations
<commit, branch, push, and PR expectations from the user; or \`None stated.\`>

## History pointers
<message ids or a \`--since-message-id\` anchor the specifier should download for context; or \`None.\`>
\`\`\`"
`;

